WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.730
The black pepper aboard the envoys' ship — the envoys kept waiting a full year at the palace gate — had rotted away in the hold until only a single bowlful was left.

2
00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:16.420
In return for that one bowl of pepper, the Sultan sent master gunners to the far side of the Indian Ocean.

3
00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:21.830
Today, in the middle of Sumatra, there stand cannon bearing the Ottoman coat of arms.

4
00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:36.400
In August of fifteen eleven, a fleet of eighteen vessels dropped anchor in the waters of the Strait of Malacca.

5
00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:46.290
On the decks stood roughly nine hundred Portuguese and some two hundred Indian soldiers; at their head was the man who read the Indian Ocean like a chessboard, Afonso de Albuquerque.

6
00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:51.220
The city facing them was one of the busiest junctions of world trade in that age.

7
00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:58.870
In Malacca, cloth merchants from Gujarat, junks carrying silk and porcelain from China, boats bringing rice from Java,

8
00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:05.720
and small sailing craft loading cloves and nutmeg from the Moluccas all lay side by side in the same harbour.

9
00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:15.930
Observers of the period wrote that dozens of languages were spoken in the city, and that within the crowd people who understood not a word of one another spoke only in the language of the scales and the purse.

10
00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:18.780
Albuquerque's aim was not to trade.

11
00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:22.110
His aim was to lock the door that trade passed through.

12
00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:29.760
For the Strait of Malacca was the only practical passage between the Indian Ocean and the South China Sea: a shallow, current-swept,

13
00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:36.450
pirate-ridden waterway that narrowed in places to just a few nautical miles between Sumatra and the Malay Peninsula.

14
00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:40.260
Spice travelled from East to West through this strait.

15
00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:43.110
Whoever held the strait held the spice.

16
00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:46.920
The city fell after several weeks of bitter street fighting.

17
00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:54.570
Sultan Mahmud Shah abandoned his elephants and his treasury and fled inland, and from there to the islands on the far side of the sea.

18
00:01:54.570 --> 00:02:01.580
At the mouth of the harbour the Portuguese raised a thick-walled fortress from material salvaged from the city's ruined stone buildings.

19
00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:05.070
The fortress was called A Famosa — the Famous.

20
00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:12.240
The guns of the Famous looked out over the harbour, and from that day on every ship entering Malacca had to anchor within their range.

21
00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:17.810
What followed was one of the most far-reaching attempts at control in the history of the Indian Ocean.

22
00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:22.100
The Portuguese established a system of passes known as the cartaz.

23
00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:31.510
Every vessel wishing to trade in the ocean had to obtain this document from a Portuguese port, call at certain harbours, and declare certain goods in certain places.

24
00:02:31.510 --> 00:02:36.760
A ship caught without the pass was confiscated along with its cargo and its crew.

25
00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:46.170
On paper this was a customs regime; at sea, it meant that an ocean no one had claimed for centuries was, for the first time, being claimed by a state.

26
00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:49.500
But the lock never quite closed.

27
00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:55.070
The fall of Malacca did not end the trade of Muslim merchants; it merely changed its address.

28
00:02:55.070 --> 00:03:03.520
Traders who had no wish to unload their goods at a port flying the Portuguese flag turned instead to the small harbours on the Sumatran side of the strait.

29
00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:10.850
And one of those harbours, a sultanate at the northern tip, grew swiftly on the wealth that came flowing in: Aceh.

30
00:03:10.850 --> 00:03:20.100
The Sultanate of Aceh, pulled together in the closing years of the fifteenth century by Ali Mughayat Shah, was no more than a small coastal principality.

31
00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:22.630
But its geography was perfect.

32
00:03:22.630 --> 00:03:30.760
It lay just outside the strait — a natural gateway for any ship wanting to reach the open Indian Ocean without touching Malacca at all.

33
00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:35.370
What is more, the foothills behind it were well suited to growing black pepper.

34
00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:46.220
By the middle of the sixteenth century Aceh had become one of the world's largest exporters of black pepper, and it was sending that pepper not along Portugal's Cape of Good Hope route, but along the old road:

35
00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:50.190
across the Indian Ocean to the Red Sea and on to Jeddah.

36
00:03:50.190 --> 00:04:00.560
By some reckonings, the quantity of pepper reaching Alexandria by way of the Red Sea in the middle of the century had come to rival the quantity rounding the Cape of Good Hope to reach Lisbon.

37
00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:03.090
The Portuguese, of course, noticed.

38
00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:10.100
The coasts of Aceh were blockaded, its ships hunted, and every sail going to or coming from Aceh became a target.

39
00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:13.270
And Aceh struck back.

40
00:04:13.270 --> 00:04:22.200
In fifteen thirty-seven and again in fifteen forty-seven it sent forces against Malacca; on both occasions it was halted before the walls of the Famous.

41
00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:25.530
The problem was not courage.

42
00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:32.860
The problem was the absence of heavy guns capable of bringing down thick stone walls — and of men who knew how to work them.

43
00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:35.710
Where was Aceh to find such men?

44
00:04:35.710 --> 00:04:39.680
It followed the direction its pepper travelled.

45
00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:45.890
Beyond the Red Sea there was, in that age, only one name that came to mind when anyone spoke of gunnery.

46
00:04:45.890 --> 00:04:51.460
To send a letter from Aceh to Istanbul is a undertaking our modern minds can scarcely grasp.

47
00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:56.230
The matter is not merely one of distance, but of the rhythm of that distance.

48
00:04:56.230 --> 00:05:02.920
From the northern tip of Sumatra to the mouth of the Red Sea, there are more than three thousand nautical miles of open ocean.

49
00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:06.730
But you cannot make that journey whenever you please.

50
00:05:06.730 --> 00:05:15.980
Trade in the Indian Ocean was bound to the calendar of the monsoon winds; for certain months of the year the wind blew one way, and for certain months it blew the other.

51
00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:22.030
Set out in the wrong season and you arrive nowhere; miss the right season and you wait six months.

52
00:05:22.030 --> 00:05:33.680
A ship leaving Aceh would catch the northeast monsoon, pass south of Ceylon, reach the coasts of Arabia, and from there make for Aden, enter the Red Sea, call at Jeddah, and put in at Suez.

53
00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:37.490
After Suez, the sea came to an end.

54
00:05:37.490 --> 00:05:44.660
Cargo and people were loaded onto camels, crossed the desert to Cairo, and from there went down the Nile to Alexandria.

55
00:05:44.660 --> 00:05:50.710
From Alexandria a galley or a galleon would carry them across the Mediterranean to Istanbul.

56
00:05:50.710 --> 00:06:00.280
If everything went well, this voyage took months; if a delay, an illness, a storm, or a pirate got in the way, it took more than a year.

57
00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:07.290
Which meant that when the sultan of Aceh wrote a letter, he had to wait two years at best for the reply to reach his hands.

58
00:06:07.290 --> 00:06:13.980
Two years is more than enough time for a siege to be won and lost, for a ruler to come to the throne and die.

59
00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:23.870
This is the real tragedy of sixteenth-century diplomacy: even when both sides genuinely wanted to help one another, the speed of communication was itself an enemy.

60
00:06:23.870 --> 00:06:26.560
Even so, Aceh tried.

61
00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:29.730
And not once, but again and again.

62
00:06:29.730 --> 00:06:35.620
From fifteen thirty-nine onward, the throne of Aceh was held by Alauddin Riayat Shah al-Kahhar.

63
00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:43.590
His epithet, al-Kahhar, meant the subduer, the one who forces submission, and he was a hard enough ruler to earn it.

64
00:06:43.590 --> 00:06:49.960
He annexed the neighbouring principalities to Aceh, built a navy, enlarged his palace.

65
00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:54.410
But on the question of the Portuguese, he knew he was not enough on his own.

66
00:06:54.410 --> 00:06:59.500
In the very first years of the fifteen sixties, envoys from Aceh reached Istanbul.

67
00:06:59.500 --> 00:07:05.710
Portuguese sources speak of a delegation from Sumatra being received at the Ottoman court in those years.

68
00:07:05.710 --> 00:07:18.960
In fact the Ottoman presence in the Indian Ocean goes back much further than that; the Red Sea fleet, Basra, Aden, the shipyard at Suez, the Indian campaigns — all of it was half a century of accumulated effort.

69
00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:27.250
There are also accounts of Ottoman gunners, Ottoman weapons, and Ottoman shipwrights filtering one by one into the far corners of the Indian Ocean.

70
00:07:27.250 --> 00:07:38.100
The Portuguese traveller Fernão Mendes Pinto writes that Turkish soldiers were present in Sumatran waters; but Pinto's travel account was a book famous for its exaggerations even in its own day,

71
00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:41.270
and the figures he gives should be read with caution.

72
00:07:41.270 --> 00:07:50.520
For the peoples of the region, the distinction was simple: wherever they came from, these soldiers arriving from the eastern Mediterranean were always given the same name.

73
00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:52.090
Rum.

74
00:07:52.090 --> 00:07:56.860
In the coastal villages of Sumatra, the Ottomans were known by that name.

75
00:07:56.860 --> 00:08:00.990
And then came the document that still sits in our hands today.

76
00:08:00.990 --> 00:08:06.880
In the Topkapı Palace Archive there is a letter registered under the number E eight thousand and nine.

77
00:08:06.880 --> 00:08:11.170
It bears the date the seventh of January, fifteen sixty-six.

78
00:08:11.170 --> 00:08:16.260
It is the letter written by Sultan Alauddin of Aceh to Suleiman the Magnificent.

79
00:08:16.260 --> 00:08:23.270
The text lay largely silent in the archive until it was published in nineteen sixty-three by the historian Razaulhak Şah.

80
00:08:23.270 --> 00:08:29.640
The language of the letter is not that of a state correspondence between equals, one ruler writing to another.

81
00:08:29.640 --> 00:08:34.570
It is the language of someone who has come to the door, writing in self-abasement.

82
00:08:34.570 --> 00:08:41.100
The sultan of Aceh describes his own country as a village of the Ottomans, and himself as a servant of the sultan.

83
00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:47.790
He tells of his war with the Portuguese, of the desperate straits he is in, of being left alone among his enemies.

84
00:08:47.790 --> 00:08:59.120
He says that Muslim pilgrims on their way to the Hajj are being raided by Portuguese ships, and that for this reason help is needed not only for Aceh but for the safety of the pilgrimage route itself.

85
00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:10.770
The items he asks for are precise: experienced commanders to teach the art of war, heavy guns that can batter fortress walls, craftsmen who know shipbuilding, and materials for fortifications.

86
00:09:10.770 --> 00:09:13.140
The letter set out.

87
00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:19.190
It waited for the monsoon, crossed the ocean, crossed the desert, crossed the Mediterranean.

88
00:09:19.190 --> 00:09:24.760
And when it reached Istanbul, the man it was written to was no longer alive.

89
00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:34.010
Suleiman the Magnificent died in September fifteen sixty-six, before the walls of the fortress of Szigetvár, in his tent, with the siege not yet over.

90
00:09:34.010 --> 00:09:41.980
The recipient of the pepper-laden ships that had sailed from Sumatra, and of that letter, had passed into history while the journey was still under way.

91
00:09:41.980 --> 00:09:48.350
The one who would give the answer was to be his son, Selim the Second, newly seated on the throne.

92
00:09:48.350 --> 00:09:57.120
The story told about what became of the delegation Aceh sent to Istanbul survived not in archive documents but in the memory of the people of Aceh.

93
00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:02.850
For centuries it was passed from father to son, and in the end it took the shape of a legend.

94
00:10:02.850 --> 00:10:08.900
And legends sometimes tell us something the documents cannot: the weight of an event upon human beings.

95
00:10:08.900 --> 00:10:11.110
The story goes like this.

96
00:10:11.110 --> 00:10:17.640
The sultan of Aceh, asking the Ottoman sultan for cannon, does not wish to arrive empty-handed.

97
00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:23.210
He loads his country's most precious commodity, black pepper, onto his ships.

98
00:10:23.210 --> 00:10:31.340
This is no ordinary gift; it is the whole wealth of Aceh, all of its bargaining power and all of its prestige, made material.

99
00:10:31.340 --> 00:10:34.670
A shipload of pepper was a fortune in that age.

100
00:10:34.670 --> 00:10:40.560
The delegation sets out and, after a voyage lasting months, arrives in Istanbul.

101
00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:44.050
But when they get there, they find no door open to them.

102
00:10:44.050 --> 00:10:47.380
The imperial capital is busy with other matters.

103
00:10:47.380 --> 00:10:54.230
There is war in the Mediterranean, there is a campaign in Europe, the court is caught up in its own commotion.

104
00:10:54.230 --> 00:11:02.360
Very few people know where this place called Sumatra even is, and the troubles of envoys from that place sit at the very bottom of Istanbul's agenda.

105
00:11:02.360 --> 00:11:04.410
The envoys wait.

106
00:11:04.410 --> 00:11:07.420
This is the most poignant part of the legend.

107
00:11:07.420 --> 00:11:09.630
They wait, and they wait.

108
00:11:09.630 --> 00:11:12.640
Months pass, the seasons turn.

109
00:11:12.640 --> 00:11:23.970
The pepper they brought is sold off, a little at a time, first just to keep themselves fed; then it is handed out, then given away as gifts, then some of it rots and some of it simply disappears.

110
00:11:23.970 --> 00:11:32.100
When at last the day comes that they are received in the sultan's presence, all that remains of a shipload of treasure is one cupak of pepper.

111
00:11:32.100 --> 00:11:37.990
The cupak is a small measure of volume used in the Malay world; an amount you might call a handful.

112
00:11:37.990 --> 00:11:41.800
The envoys offer up that single handful of pepper.

113
00:11:41.800 --> 00:11:48.330
According to the story, the sultan looks not at the smallness of the gift before him, but at the journey behind it.

114
00:11:48.330 --> 00:11:58.540
These are people who set out from the other end of the world, from a country whose name he had barely heard, who came all this way, who waited years at his gate, who spent everything they had in the waiting,

115
00:11:58.540 --> 00:12:02.030
and who still did not give up on presenting their gift.

116
00:12:02.030 --> 00:12:05.040
What they ask for is granted.

117
00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:10.130
Cannon are cast, master gunners are assigned, and they are sent to Aceh.

118
00:12:10.130 --> 00:12:14.100
And one of those cannon is given a name in Aceh: Lada Sicupak.

119
00:12:14.100 --> 00:12:19.190
That is, "one cupak of pepper." A handful of black pepper.

120
00:12:19.190 --> 00:12:26.520
In telling this story, one thing has to be said plainly: the Ottoman documents we possess make no mention of any such scene.

121
00:12:26.520 --> 00:12:33.050
In the Mühimme registers there is nothing about waiting envoys, rotting pepper, or a sultan moved to feeling.

122
00:12:33.050 --> 00:12:40.220
What the documents contain is the cold language of the state: request, decision, allocation, appointment.

123
00:12:40.220 --> 00:12:46.270
The details of the legend were probably added later, as the memory of the event took shape in Aceh.

124
00:12:46.270 --> 00:12:49.920
But the legend does capture one thing correctly.

125
00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:54.210
For Aceh, this really did mean staking everything it had.

126
00:12:54.210 --> 00:12:58.820
And for Istanbul, this really was an item at the very bottom of the agenda.

127
00:12:58.820 --> 00:13:04.230
That gulf between the weight each side gave the matter is also the key to what would happen next.

128
00:13:04.230 --> 00:13:12.520
Because while Aceh waited for the help that would save its life, a far closer and far more searing problem was about to appear in front of Istanbul.

129
00:13:12.520 --> 00:13:16.330
Istanbul did not refuse Aceh's request.

130
00:13:16.330 --> 00:13:21.420
On the contrary, on paper it gave an extremely serious response.

131
00:13:21.420 --> 00:13:30.350
By a decree dated the twenty-first of September, fifteen sixty-seven, Kurdoğlu Hızır Reis was appointed to command the fleet that would be sent to Aceh.

132
00:13:30.350 --> 00:13:36.080
He was a seaman who had spent his life in the waters of the Red Sea and knew the region.

133
00:13:36.080 --> 00:13:45.010
The records in Mühimme Register number seven show the scale of the force being prepared: fifteen galleys and two barks were to be fitted out at the Suez arsenal.

134
00:13:45.010 --> 00:13:49.940
A chief gunner and seven master gunners were assigned from the imperial artillery corps.

135
00:13:49.940 --> 00:13:58.870
Among the weapons to be loaded were heavy guns of the badaluşka, şayka and hevâyî types; the badaluşka was a long-barrelled gun for battering fortress walls,

136
00:13:58.870 --> 00:14:03.800
while the hevâyî was a mortar-type piece that dropped its shot behind the wall on a high arc.

137
00:14:03.800 --> 00:14:08.890
In other words, what was being sent was no ornament; it was a siege battery.

138
00:14:08.890 --> 00:14:16.060
In addition to these, troops from Egypt, sappers, and materiel for use in a fortress siege were allocated.

139
00:14:16.060 --> 00:14:23.230
The justification for the decision stated that meeting the request was an obligation both of religion and of the traditions of the state.

140
00:14:23.230 --> 00:14:27.520
Anyone looking at the document might think the matter was settled.

141
00:14:27.520 --> 00:14:34.210
A fleet has been prepared, its commander appointed, its guns loaded, its destination fixed.

142
00:14:34.210 --> 00:14:38.340
But at exactly that moment, Yemen rose in revolt.

143
00:14:38.340 --> 00:14:44.230
The rebellion that began in fifteen sixty-seven in the mountainous interior of Yemen grew quickly.

144
00:14:44.230 --> 00:14:50.280
The rebels seized the mountain passes, besieged the Ottoman garrisons, and Sanaa fell.

145
00:14:50.280 --> 00:14:56.010
Within a short time the Ottoman presence in Yemen was squeezed into a handful of coastal fortresses.

146
00:14:56.010 --> 00:14:59.820
And Yemen was no ordinary province.

147
00:14:59.820 --> 00:15:06.670
It was the place that controlled the mouth of the Red Sea, Aden, the entire gateway opening onto the Indian Ocean.

148
00:15:06.670 --> 00:15:12.720
If Yemen were lost, the fleet being prepared at Suez would have nowhere left to go.

149
00:15:12.720 --> 00:15:16.210
The priority changed.

150
00:15:16.210 --> 00:15:23.380
The fleet bound for Sumatra was turned not toward the direction it was meant to sail, but north and east, toward the coasts of Yemen.

151
00:15:23.380 --> 00:15:30.070
The galleys, together with part of the troops and guns meant for Aceh, entered the war in southern Arabia.

152
00:15:30.070 --> 00:15:37.400
It took the Ottomans a long and bloody series of operations, over the course of several years, before they could take Yemen back.

153
00:15:37.400 --> 00:15:41.850
We do not know exactly what became of Kurdoğlu Hızır Reis.

154
00:15:41.850 --> 00:15:47.740
In the records we have, there is no trace of his setting out for Sumatra after the Yemen campaign.

155
00:15:47.740 --> 00:15:51.230
He most likely died during those operations.

156
00:15:51.230 --> 00:15:55.200
The man appointed to go to Aceh never saw Aceh.

157
00:15:55.200 --> 00:16:01.410
What reached Sumatra was not the fleet that had been prepared, but a very small fragment broken off from it.

158
00:16:01.410 --> 00:16:05.700
The sources speak of two ships arriving in Aceh.

159
00:16:05.700 --> 00:16:10.310
Two ships are nothing beside a fleet of fifteen galleys and two barks.

160
00:16:10.310 --> 00:16:15.080
But what those two ships carried should be measured not in tonnage, but in knowledge.

161
00:16:15.080 --> 00:16:19.050
Because it was not only cannons that came down from those ships.

162
00:16:19.050 --> 00:16:25.740
Masters who knew how to cast cannon came ashore, along with gunsmiths and engineers trained in fortification.

163
00:16:25.740 --> 00:16:33.070
And what that means is this: what arrived in Aceh was not a one-time gift, but a repeatable capability.

164
00:16:33.070 --> 00:16:38.800
If they give you a cannon, you have a cannon; if that cannon bursts, you have nothing left.

165
00:16:38.800 --> 00:16:42.930
But if they teach you how to cast cannon, you have an industry.

166
00:16:42.930 --> 00:16:46.100
Foundries were established in Aceh.

167
00:16:46.100 --> 00:16:52.150
Local copper and tin, local labor, and Ottoman knowledge of moulds and alloys all came together.

168
00:16:52.150 --> 00:16:59.480
At the northern tip of Sumatra, in a place no European state had reckoned with, heavy siege guns began to be cast.

169
00:16:59.480 --> 00:17:05.530
This production did not die with the masters who had brought it; it carried on for generations.

170
00:17:05.530 --> 00:17:13.980
At the beginning of the seventeenth century, it is recorded that the Acehnese arsenal held some twelve hundred cannon and around eight hundred swivel guns.

171
00:17:13.980 --> 00:17:18.430
That is a figure no other local state in the region came anywhere near.

172
00:17:18.430 --> 00:17:23.040
Istanbul was never able to send the fleet it had promised Aceh.

173
00:17:23.040 --> 00:17:29.730
But in place of the fleet it could not send, it sent — without realizing it — something far more lasting.

174
00:17:29.730 --> 00:17:36.260
And Aceh decided to test this new power, for the first time, at its enemy's strongest point.

175
00:17:36.260 --> 00:17:41.350
In the year fifteen sixty-eight, the Acehnese fleet set sail for Malacca.

176
00:17:41.350 --> 00:17:47.240
The numbers given by Portuguese chroniclers are quite striking as to the size of the force they faced.

177
00:17:47.240 --> 00:17:54.090
According to the accounts, the Acehnese army numbered fifteen thousand men, and it brought with it some two hundred cannon.

178
00:17:54.090 --> 00:18:00.140
And manning those guns was a group the sources point to insistently: around four hundred Ottoman gunners.

179
00:18:00.140 --> 00:18:12.110
The Portuguese refer to them in their own records simply as "the Turks." One has to keep in mind that these figures come from enemy sources, and that enemy sources tend to inflate the other side.

180
00:18:12.110 --> 00:18:18.960
But the course of the siege shows, at the very least, that the gunnery was conducted with serious professional skill.

181
00:18:18.960 --> 00:18:22.450
The man defending Malacca was Dom Leonis Pereira.

182
00:18:22.450 --> 00:18:31.860
The force at his disposal was almost laughably small next to the attackers: roughly fifteen hundred fighting men, and only about two hundred of them Portuguese.

183
00:18:31.860 --> 00:18:38.070
The rest had been drawn from Malays, from mixed communities, and from the various peoples living in the city.

184
00:18:38.070 --> 00:18:41.880
Against this, Pereira had two advantages.

185
00:18:41.880 --> 00:18:50.970
The first was the walls of A Famosa; for half a century they had been steadily strengthened — a European-style fortification, thick and low in profile.

186
00:18:50.970 --> 00:18:59.900
The second was politics: the sultan of Johor, who was at least as unsettled by Aceh's rise as the Portuguese were, this time sided with Malacca.

187
00:18:59.900 --> 00:19:04.350
The Muslim powers of the strait could not unite against their common enemy.

188
00:19:04.350 --> 00:19:10.560
When the siege began, a cannonade unlike anything it had seen before came down on Malacca's walls.

189
00:19:10.560 --> 00:19:15.170
The Acehnese batteries fired at high volume and at regular intervals.

190
00:19:15.170 --> 00:19:20.100
This was nothing like the scattered, irregular bombardments of earlier attempts.

191
00:19:20.100 --> 00:19:22.310
Then came the assault.

192
00:19:22.310 --> 00:19:29.480
The Acehnese had brought some two hundred ladders to set against the walls; that alone shows how carefully the attack had been planned.

193
00:19:29.480 --> 00:19:33.770
The ladders went up against the walls, and the soldiers climbed.

194
00:19:33.770 --> 00:19:37.900
And those who climbed never made it over the top.

195
00:19:37.900 --> 00:19:41.710
The defenders toppled the ladders and threw the climbers down.

196
00:19:41.710 --> 00:19:46.640
The low, thick profile of the walls made it difficult for cannonballs to open a breach.

197
00:19:46.640 --> 00:19:52.530
Then Pereira made the riskiest move in the defender's book: he opened the gates and went out.

198
00:19:52.530 --> 00:19:58.100
Portuguese and Malay soldiers plunged into the trenches and gun positions the Acehnese had dug.

199
00:19:58.100 --> 00:20:01.110
Many men died in the close fighting.

200
00:20:01.110 --> 00:20:06.360
As they pulled back, they carried off swords, muskets, and one cannon.

201
00:20:06.360 --> 00:20:09.690
The siege lasted three days.

202
00:20:09.690 --> 00:20:14.140
At the end of the third day, the Acehnese army returned to its ships.

203
00:20:14.140 --> 00:20:18.590
Here lies the bitter irony in the story of those guns and those gunners.

204
00:20:18.590 --> 00:20:27.040
Aceh spent years asking for help from the other side of the world, spent a fortune, and let its envoys grow old waiting at foreign doors.

205
00:20:27.040 --> 00:20:30.690
In the end, it got the knowledge and the weapons it wanted.

206
00:20:30.690 --> 00:20:38.020
And in the first great test it took those weapons to, it turned back in under three days against a garrison a tenth its size.

207
00:20:38.020 --> 00:20:47.270
Because winning a siege is not only a matter of cannon; it is a matter of supply, of time, of control of the sea, of allies, and of patience.

208
00:20:47.270 --> 00:20:52.840
Aceh had the guns, but it did not have command of the strait, and it did not have time.

209
00:20:52.840 --> 00:20:56.170
Malacca never did fall into Aceh's hands.

210
00:20:56.170 --> 00:21:00.940
It was attempted twice more in the fifteen seventies, without success.

211
00:21:00.940 --> 00:21:06.350
Portugal finally lost Malacca in sixteen forty-one — but not to Aceh.

212
00:21:06.350 --> 00:21:09.360
To the Dutch, allied with Johor.

213
00:21:09.360 --> 00:21:14.450
Even so, that slender thread running from Istanbul to Sumatra was never cut.

214
00:21:14.450 --> 00:21:20.500
At the Acehnese court, the Ottomans went on being remembered as something more than an ally.

215
00:21:20.500 --> 00:21:30.390
The history written in Aceh in the seventeenth century, the Bustan us-Salatin — the Garden of Kings — records this relationship as part of the sultanate's founding story.

216
00:21:30.390 --> 00:21:35.320
Aceh's rulers used a language that cast them as bound to a distant caliph.

217
00:21:35.320 --> 00:21:40.090
The Ottoman crescent and its emblems worked their way onto Aceh's banners and seals.

218
00:21:40.090 --> 00:21:45.500
And that memory returned to the stage once more, three centuries later.

219
00:21:45.500 --> 00:21:52.990
When the Netherlands declared war on Aceh in eighteen seventy-three, Aceh knocked on that very same door again.

220
00:21:52.990 --> 00:22:04.000
The envoy the Sultan sent, Habib Abdurrahman az-Zahir, travelled to Istanbul and delivered Aceh's request for protection; he presented documents declaring that the country,

221
00:22:04.000 --> 00:22:07.970
its people and its ruler surrendered themselves to the Caliph.

222
00:22:07.970 --> 00:22:14.020
But the Ottoman Empire of eighteen seventy-three was not the Ottoman Empire of fifteen sixty-seven.

223
00:22:14.020 --> 00:22:24.070
It was under heavy pressure on its own borders, hemmed in by the diplomatic objections of the European powers, and sending a fleet to the far side of the Indian Ocean was simply out of the question.

224
00:22:24.070 --> 00:22:27.080
The only thing granted was moral support.

225
00:22:27.080 --> 00:22:33.450
Az-Zahir returned empty-handed, and in Aceh he took up arms and fought at the front himself.

226
00:22:33.450 --> 00:22:41.580
When the Dutch army captured the palace in Aceh's capital in eighteen seventy-four, among the spoils it found there was an enormous bronze cannon.

227
00:22:41.580 --> 00:22:49.390
Four point one five meters long, with a bore of eighteen point five centimeters, weighing five thousand six hundred kilos.

228
00:22:49.390 --> 00:22:52.880
The Acehnese called it Lada Sicupak.

229
00:22:52.880 --> 00:22:54.930
A handful of black pepper.

230
00:22:54.930 --> 00:23:05.300
The cannon was carried off to the Netherlands as a trophy of war, and to this day it still stands there, in the museum of Bronbeek, the old soldiers' home in the city of Arnhem.

231
00:23:05.300 --> 00:23:09.110
And here is the most surprising part of the story.

232
00:23:09.110 --> 00:23:19.800
In their study of the Arabic inscriptions on the cannon, the researchers Claude Guillot and Ludvig Kalus concluded that it had most likely been cast not in Istanbul, but in India, in Gujarat.

233
00:23:19.800 --> 00:23:27.610
In other words, the object Aceh had kept for centuries as "the cannon Istanbul sent us" had probably never passed through Istanbul at all.

234
00:23:27.610 --> 00:23:32.860
But this does not make the story a lie; it shows us where the story truly lives.

235
00:23:32.860 --> 00:23:37.630
What the Ottomans actually sent to Aceh was never a cannon in the first place.

236
00:23:37.630 --> 00:23:46.400
Of the fifteen galleys that set out, only two ships reached Sumatra, and what mattered was the knowledge left behind by the craftsmen who stepped ashore from them.

237
00:23:46.400 --> 00:23:51.810
What was received in return for a handful of black pepper was not a weapon, but a craft.

238
00:23:51.810 --> 00:24:00.580
And the people of Aceh named every cannon that craft produced, regardless of where each one had been cast, after the memory of that door on the other side of the world.

239
00:24:00.580 --> 00:24:04.550
That brings us to the end of today's episode of Neuron History.

240
00:24:04.550 --> 00:24:08.680
Until we meet again, in a new story.
